The Second Treaty of Paris (1815) set France's indemnity at **700 million francs**, payable over five years. This is the standard historical figure found in references on the Congress of Vienna settlement. The proposed answer of 1 billion francs is incorrect, and its explanation (invoking an Allied finance commission's preliminary assessment) is a fabricated justification — the 700 million figure is the actual treaty amount, not a misreading.

France did pay it off early — via loans arranged by Ouvrard with Baring Brothers and Hope & Co., completed by 1818 at the Congress of Aix-la-Chapelle — that part of the context is accurate, but the indemnity amount stated is wrong. The 5-billion-franc figure indeed belongs to 1871.

